		"nextNodeId": 77,
		"nodes": {
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "METRONOME\nThe Metronome is one of the simplest ways to generate a steady flow of triggers for your patch. The interval between the triggers can be set with the frequency(in Hz). Besides triggers you can also use the phase of the metronome to modulate other nodes. This sends out values running from 0 to 1. The 1 is sent when the trigger is sent. This can be seen as a very crude Saw Oscillator. Lastly there are options to reset the Metronome and you can manually disable the Metronome as well. The example patch is quite large because we would like to demonstrate different nodes that can make use of triggers.\n\n- Patch Explained -\n\n• The Metronome is toggled on and off with a Bool. Because this is an input node the user will see this when using the patch in Resolume as an effect or source.\n• At the top we have a Triangle that gets and Edge and is sent to the Transform to be modulated further. Lastly it is sent to the Shape Render where it gets its color.\n• The Phase of the Metronome is “reversed” by using the X-1 node, running from 1 to 0 into the thickness of the edge, creating a fading effect.\n• The Random nodes modulate the X and Y position of the Triangle. A smooth node is added for a smooth transition between the positions.\n• The Attack Release gets triggered and modulates the scale of the triangle. The Add node is added so the scale never becomes 0.\n• The Bag node holds the value 0, 1 and 2, which are chosen randomly, but never twice until all values are used. The modulates the rotation, smooth is again added for smoothing the motion.\n• The count output of the Metronome node increases its value by 1 on each trigger. \n• The Wrap node takes the count output and wraps the value between 0 and 3, basically creating a counter from 0 to 3.\n• This is used to cycle through the 4 different colors inside the Switch node. More smoothing is used to smooth the transition between hues."
					}
				},
